  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic linking underlying DNA sequence to chromatin organization and a broader epigenetic pattern. At the top, a DNA double helix is wrapped into nucleosomes to form chromatin. In the central region, different DNA-binding factors interact with this chromatin: proteins that recognize AT-rich stretches, a CpG-binding factor, general transcription factors, and a chromatin “modifier.” These factors are shown bound at distinct sequence contexts and appear to recruit or reinforce local chromatin states around nearby nucleosomes.\n\nAcross the locus, colored halos indicate different local regulatory environments, suggesting that sequence-specific binders and modifiers create domain-like chromatin features rather than uniform packaging. The bottom “epigenetic landscape” condenses this into a long stretch of chromatin marked by broad colored regions, implying that local DNA composition helps establish extended chromatin states.\n\nThe graph at the bottom shows AT content varying across the same genomic region, with plateaus and a dip. The overall message is that changes in base composition, especially AT-rich versus less AT-rich segments, correlate with recruitment of distinct DNA-binding proteins and modifiers, which in turn shape chromatin structure and the larger-scale epigenetic landscape.",
